{"acceptedResponses":["B"],"answer":"B","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(1\\dfrac34\\)","B":"\\(4\\)","C":"\\(6\\)","D":"\\(12\\)","E":"\\(-4\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1966_AMC_12_Q16","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["math:real-exponent-laws"],"contentHash":"85200f41d5786bc511a283706a4892fbde3ec44036113efd1902c54e78dbdf7d","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reduced-system-substitution","label":"Common bases and substitution","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduced-system-substitution":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reduced-system-elimination","label":"Common bases and elimination","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduced-system-elimination":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_090"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"logarithmic-system-substitution","label":"Take logarithms and substitution","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-system-substitution":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_089"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"logarithmic-system-elimination","label":"Take logarithms and elimination","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-system-elimination":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_090"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h041","cnm_042"],"id":"common-bases-and-substitution","label":"Rewrite with common bases","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-bases-and-substitution":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089","cnm_h041","cnm_042"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_h041","cnm_042"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":16,"question":"If\n\n\\[ \\begin{gathered} \\dfrac{4^x}{2^{x+y}}=8,\\\\ \\dfrac{9^{x+y}}{3^{5y}}=243, \\end{gathered} \\]\n\n\\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are real numbers, then \\(xy\\) equals:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"Since \\(4=2^2\\) and \\(9=3^2\\), rewriting each equation with a single base will turn its exponent into a linear expression. All bases are positive, so the power laws apply to the real exponents, and neither denominator can be zero.\n\nThe equations become\n\\[\n2^{2x-(x+y)}=2^3,\n\\qquad\n3^{2(x+y)-5y}=3^5,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n2^{x-y}=2^3,\n\\qquad\n3^{2x-3y}=3^5.\n\\]\nPowers with base \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) strictly increase as the exponent increases. Thus equal powers with either base must have equal exponents:\n\\[\nx-y=3,\n\\qquad\n2x-3y=5.\n\\]\nThe first equation gives \\(x=y+3\\). Substituting into the second gives\n\\[\n2(y+3)-3y=5,\n\\qquad\n6-y=5,\n\\]\nso \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=4\\). Therefore \\(xy=\\boxed{4}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089"],"explanation":"Since \\(4=2^2\\) and \\(9=3^2\\), rewriting each equation with a single base will turn its exponent into a linear expression. All bases are positive, so the power laws apply to the real exponents, and neither denominator can be zero.\n\nThe equations become\n\\[\n2^{2x-(x+y)}=2^3,\n\\qquad\n3^{2(x+y)-5y}=3^5,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n2^{x-y}=2^3,\n\\qquad\n3^{2x-3y}=3^5.\n\\]\nPowers with base \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) strictly increase as the exponent increases. Thus equal powers with either base must have equal exponents:\n\\[\nx-y=3,\n\\qquad\n2x-3y=5.\n\\]\nThe first equation gives \\(x=y+3\\). Substituting into the second gives\n\\[\n2(y+3)-3y=5,\n\\qquad\n6-y=5,\n\\]\nso \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=4\\). Therefore \\(xy=\\boxed{4}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).","id":"common-bases-and-substitution","label":"Rewrite with common bases","legacyRouteId":"common-bases-and-substitution","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted pri